> Yeah.  See, a certain amount of polypiling is one of the "fun" things
> about Nethack -- if you're clever, you wedge a bunch of stuff up and get
> a whole bunch of new stuff, totally at random.  Eliminating the wand
> would be a little extreme, I think.

I agree entirely. I remember that was one of my "wow" moments when i
first started playing, thinking to myself "i wonder what happens if i
zap polymorph at some of these crappy scrolls".

Still, i think that polypiling as it stands now is a little broken,
partly because it's too powerful, but moreso because it encourages
tedious play. Polypiling is so useful that if I don't use a wand to
its fullest (carefully arranging items into stacks with even
distribution of materials), I almost feel like I'm playing a conduct
game.

I think one solution might be to make the wand/spell of polymorph not
affect more than one square. It could be limited to an adjacent
square, or it could follow a vector in the direction you zap until it
finds a non-empty square and then polymorph everything on that square.
Shudder and golem rules could be left as is (although some new types
of golems would be a bonus too).

Another option would be to make it so that the effect remains exactly
as is for monsters, but items can only be hit with a downwards zap,
thus effectively limiting item polymorphing to a single square. After
all, the level at which one would point a wand to zap a dragon is very
different to the level one would point it to hit a key on the same
square. This would probably be easier to code than the other option,
but the obvious downside of this is that the poly-item efect would be
harder to find independently for the non-spoiled.

One other thought on increasing the risk of poly zaps: every zap could
have a small chance of generating an air or earth elemental ("The very
air changes character and draws itself together"), or a water or fire
if water/lava squares are nearby. Elementals are generally scarier
than golems, especially since people polypile iron and stone things
less often than scrolls and spellbooks. Also, it would be neat if
golems generated from a pile of exclusively blessed objects had a
chance of generating tame, but then that kind of takes away from the
risk factor...

just some ideas.



